From: "R. David Murray" <rdmurray@keene.edu>
Date: Tue, 12 Apr 1994 09:43:10 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-4-73] BUSI:Public Access Unix Venture Help Wanted...

On Sun, 20 Mar 1994, Phil G. Fraering wrote:
> For about the past month or two in our spare time (not much!) a friend
> of mine and I have been brainstorming the concept of setting up a
> public access unix node in the Lafayette, Louisiana area. Does anyone
> here have any experience in that area, or any ideas for unexplored
> business opportunities in this area?

There is lots and lots of this going on right now.  I don't think the 
profit margins are very large, but if nothing else it sure is a great way 
to pay for your own bandwidth.  

Not speaking from direct experience, but after having done some research, 
I'd agree with your other contacts.  After sinking the cost of the 
platform ($4-$5K for a 486PC running BSDI, for example), the modem pool 
is the largest expense, and the one you have to watch carefully.  It's 
bad for business if your modem pool rings busy very often, so you need to 
watch your utilization and buy more phone lines and modems *before* you 
run out of capacity.  The modems themselves aren't really that expensive, 
but you've got to pay for the phone lines, and that's a monthly charge.
But vis the modems, you will want to buy top of the line, because you 
need to be interoperable with the maximum number of other modems.  And, 
of course, you'll want to have a significant portion of your modem pool 
be the highest speed modems available on the market. 

Oh, if your user base gets above 5 or 10 simultaneous users, you're
going to need a second machine to dedicate to news service and other
servers (I'm pretty sure). 

As I say, I don't have any direct experience in this area, but if things 
go along as I plan for them to, I will by the end of the year.  I'm 
working with an entrepreneaur to set up a public access unix site here in 
Keene, NH.  Right now we are kind of in a holding pattern while I help a 
local coalition of schools, businesses, and other organizations set up a 
network provider operation.  (No network provider has a POP in the local 
calling area, and the major regional Internet provider would not agree to 
allow us to resell SLIP connections (they may have changed their tune but 
it may be too late for them now), so we've put together a business plan 
for setting up our own NOC and reselling bandwidth purchased from Sprint.)

All this because when I moved to this region I found I couldn't get 
Internet access via a local phone call.  I just had to do something about 
that <grin>.

As an Extropian I'm in kind of an uncomfortable situation here.  The
organization I am currently working for (this coalition) is seeking NII
funding to connect the local schools.  I really hate the idea of using
federal tax receipts for something I'm involved in, but the entrepreneaur
I'm working with is not himself an Extropian and so isn't willing to go
ahead without waiting for this coalition to build the local Internet
access point.  Besides, he's the business head, so he's probably right,
strategically. 

Well, whatever else happens, I'm certainly getting a good education out 
of this.  

> pool.  I also doubt there will be *that* many individual users in the
> Lafayette area (although I could be wrong). I would probably be
> relying on selling backdoor SLIP access in order to stay in the black.

We're thinking that the costs of supplying SLIP access are not very 
different from the costs of supplying a login account.  Since we face 
potential competition in the region (there is another login account/slip 
provider well established in the next metropolitan area to the east, and 
he is expanding in our direction, though he has chosen not to compete 
directly, for the moment, with this coalition), we are thinking of 
offering SLIP connections at close to the same price as login accounts.
My personal prediction is that medium term there is going to be a 
significant relative growth in SLIP (rather, probably, PPP) connections 
vs login accounts (but both will continue their explosive growth).

As for potential usage...I have seen estimates that personal computer 
penetration in the populace has risen from 30% a couple years ago to 
almost 50% today.  Of those, less than half have modems (I think, I don't 
have a reference handy).  Of *those*, figure you can pick up, what, say a 
quarter?  So with good advertising you have a potential (medium term) user 
base of somewhere around 5% of the population.  Note that that well 
established login account provider I mentioned above has been seeing 
growth rates comparable to Internet growth rates overall.

I've seen estimates that 1 modem per 100 accounts is an absolute minimum,
with 1/45 more reasonable (and 1/10 if you offer a "business plan" where 
the user is almost guaranteed access).  Of course, for the user who 
really needs guaranteed access, you charge him enough to cover the costs 
of a dedicated phone line, modem, and port.

> 1. Information filtering. There's a mountain of information driving past on

I think that, until AI gets really good, there ought to be a market for 
*human* information filters.  Charge a very modest subscription fee for a 
focused digest.  For example, I'd pay $10 or $15 a year for someone to 
feed me weekly summaries of "important" Internet events.  (Important 
defined by the editor, and if I find myself in disagreement I give 
feedback, and if I still disagree, I drop my subscription).  Find enough 
clients, and this should pay for the hours it takes to compile (some 
number of which hours you'd spend perusing the Net anyway).

>    a. The Extropians List Software!
>       My main idea here is to offer an Extropians-software-mediated echo 
>       of such high-volume mailing lists as the linux mailing lists,
>       the cypherpunks mailing list, and others.

I won't pay for this.  I find the Extropian list features useful for the
Extropians list, which I (used to) find I read more of than not. The list
software lets me cut down the noise.  But for the other lists, they are
mostly noise to me, so my technique is to scan the list of postings and
select only those items that look interesting.  Yes, I could do this with
exclude all, but that simply is not as easy as scanning subjects in the
mailbox and deleting everything afterwards, or better yet using a
newsreader where the mailing list is gatewayed to a local newsgroup. 

>    a.ii. It would be neat if I could get a portion (*) of the
>          extropians list as an anchor tenant. I think, but am not
>          sure, that Harry has been thinking of using Sameer's system,
>          if it gets set up, to handle part or all of the list.

There might also be special services you could offer Extropians that 
might make it worth our while to buy a cheap telnet account from you.  I 
think Geoff(?) was thinking along these lines earlier.  I've certainly 
got that notion in the back of my mind, though I have no firm ideas yet.  
Viva la competition <grin>.  (I take that back: there is one idea already 
floated that I'd been thinking about too: a MOO like Metaverse but 
dedicated to Extropian principles.  We'd want to figure out MOO cash 
from get-go.)

>    a.iii.Perhaps have a special "extropians" user group that could

I couldn't figure out what exactly you were talking about in this item.
Can you clarify?  If you mean, log in to your server and use news to read 
the mailing list . . . there might be some potential there, but I think 
the notion would benefit from some group-think development.

--David

